<h1>TActiveHiddenField</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.1" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TActiveHiddenField" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TActiveHiddenField</tt> is the active control counterpart of <tt>THiddenField</tt>. It
renders a hidden input field, which carries a value between the browser and the server
without showing it to the user. The <tt>Value</tt> property holds that value, and a
callback handler reads it and writes it.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TActiveHiddenField ID="Hidden1" Value="alpha" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
public function buttonCallback($sender, $param)
{
	$current = $this->Hidden1->Value;
	$this->Hidden1->Value = 'a new value';
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Writing the value during a callback</h2>

<p class="block-content">
Assigning <tt>Value</tt> during a callback also updates the input element in the browser.
The update needs the posted value of the field, so it happens only after the
<tt>OnLoad</tt> stage of the page. Code that runs earlier changes the server-side value
alone, and the browser keeps the value it already has.
</p>

<h2>The value changed event</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The control raises <tt>OnValueChanged</tt> when the value that arrives from the browser
differs from the value the control held before. A client-side script that writes to the
input therefore reaches the server on the next request, and the handler sees the new value.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="javascript" CssClass="source block-content">
document.getElementById('ctl0_body_Hidden1').value = 'set by the browser';
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
A page finds that element id through the control's <tt>ClientID</tt> property rather than
writing it out by hand.
</p>

